) for b, s in zip(bases, sides)]\r\n    except (OSError, ValueError, UnicodeDecodeError) as exc:\r\n        return str(exc)\r\n    parents = [old[0] for old in olds]\r\n    moved = _in_complete(path) and not any(_in_complete(old[1]) for old in olds)\r\n    changed = record not in parents and not (record is None and not any(parents))\r\n    if not (moved or changed or record):\r\n        return None\r\n    _drafts, request, refusal = _parse_request(text, path)\r\n    if refusal:\r\n        return refusal\r\n    is_close, invalid = close(wi, data, request) if changed else (False, None)\r\n    held = [(old[0], _carried_request(old, record)) for old in olds]\r\n    return _kadoption.record_change(held, record, request, moved, is_close, invalid)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _carried_request(old, record):\r\n    \"\"\"The request a base's spec `old` (`_spec_at`'s tuple) holds when it\r\n    carries `record`, read through the one parser; None when it carries\r\n    another record or its section does not parse (so it carries no request).\r\n\r\n    Implements: SR-148, LLR-345\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    if not record or old[0] != record:\r\n        return None\r\n    _drafts, request, refusal = _parse_request(old[3], old[1])\r\n    return None if refusal else request\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _parse_request(text, path):\r\n    \"\"\"`(drafts, adoptions, refusal)` of the spec text's `## Dispositions`\r\n    section, through the one parser; a spec absent from the tree requests\r\n    nothing.\r\n\r\n    Implements: SR-148, LLR-345\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    if text is None:\r\n        return [], [], None\r\n    import intake  # the one disposition parser's home; deferred\r\n\r\n    return intake.parse_disposition_section(text, path)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _base_spec(root, rev, paths):\r\n    \"\"\"`_spec_at` for a base: a spec whose text, front matter or record a\r\n    base holds malformed held no readable record (the tree under judgement\r\n    is read strictly, so a record cannot hide behind a malformed base, and a\r\n    commit repairing a malformed spec is not refused for its parent). A spec\r\n    git cannot show still raises OSError.\r\n\r\n    Implements: SR-148, LLR-345\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    try:\r\n        return _spec_at(root, rev, paths)\r\n    except (ValueError, UnicodeDecodeError):\r\n        return None, next(iter(paths), None), {}, None\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _spec_at(root, rev, paths):\r\n    \"\"\"`(record, path, front matter, text)` of the one spec `paths` names in\r\n    the tree `rev` (None: the index); all None when the tree holds none.\r\n    Raises ValueError when several specs carry the id or the record is\r\n    malformed, OSError or UnicodeDecodeError when the spec cannot be read.\r\n\r\n    Implements: SR-148, LLR-345\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    if not paths:\r\n        return None, None, {}, None\r\n    if len(paths) > 1:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"{} specs carry one id: {}\".format(len(paths), paths))\r\n    (path,) = paths\r\n    data = _kitgit.git_bytes(root, [\"show\", \"{}:{}\".format(rev or \"\", path)])\r\n    if data is None:\r\n        raise OSError(\"git cannot show {} at {}\".format(path, rev or \"the index\"))\r\n    text = data.decode(\"utf-8\").replace(\"\\r\\n\", \"\\n\").replace(\"\\r\", \"\\n\")\r\n    meta, _body = _kregistry.parse_spec_frontmatter(text, path)\r\n    record, refusal = _kadoption.record_ids(meta, path)\r\n    if refusal:\r\n        raise ValueError(refusal)\r\n    return record, path, meta, text\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _in_complete(path):\r\n    \"\"\"Whether `path` stands in a `complete/` folder of either spec root.\r\n\r\n    Implements: SR-148, LLR-345\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    match = _SPEC_PATH.match(path or \"\")\r\n    return bool(match) and match.group(1) == _COMPLETE\r\n\r\n\r\ndef staged_adoption_record_lines(root):\r\n    \"\"\"`commit_adoption_record_lines` for the commit being made: the index\r\n    against HEAD, and against MERGE_HEAD while a merge is in progress. Before\r\n    the first commit nothing is judged.\r\n\r\n    A SQUASH LANDING (git's `SQUASH_MSG`, read by `kitlib.git.squash_commits`)\r\n    whose tip contains HEAD is judged by its lane commits: each commit\r\n    HEAD..tip is judged by this rule (`_squash_walk`), and the index is\r\n    judged against the tip too, so a staged spec equal to the tip's own is\r\n    carried and any other staged record change is refused. A squash whose tip\r\n    does not contain HEAD is judged like any commit, its refusal ending with\r\n    `SQUASH_HINT`.\r\n\r\n    Implements: SR-148, LLR-345\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    head = (\r\n        _kitgit.git_out(root, [\"rev-parse\", \"--verify\", \"--quiet\", \"HEAD\"]) or \"\"\r\n    ).strip()\r\n    if not head:\r\n        return []\r\n    merging = (\r\n        _kitgit.git_out(root, [\"rev-parse\", \"-q\", \"--verify\", \"MERGE_HEAD\"]) or \"\"\r\n    ).strip()\r\n    if merging:\r\n        return _record_lines(root, [head, merging], None, _not_a_close)\r\n    squashed = _kitgit.squash_commits(root)\r\n    tip = squashed[0] if squashed else None\r\n    within = (\r\n        tip and _kitgit.git_out(root, [\"merge-base\", \"--is-ancestor\", head, tip]) == \"\"\r\n    )\r\n    if not within:\r\n        lines = _record_lines(root, [head], None, _not_a_close)\r\n        return lines + [SQUASH_HINT] if lines and squashed else lines\r\n    walked = _squash_walk(root, head, tip)\r\n    return walked + _record_lines(root, [head, tip], None, _not_a_close)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _squash_walk(root, head, tip):\r\n    \"\"\"The lines of every commit `head..tip`, oldest first, each judged by\r\n    `commit_adoption_record_lines` against its own parents, as the merge\r\n    slot's lane-commit walk judges it with this rule as a rider; a line when\r\n    git cannot list the range. The commits are walked here, not through\r\n    `text_then_act.lane_commit_walk`, because that module imports this one\r\n    for `LANE_COMMIT_RIDERS`, and the import graph admits no cycle.\r\n\r\n    Implements: SR-148, LLR-345\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    commits = _kitgit.git_out(root, [\"rev-list\", \"--reverse\", head + \"..\" + tip])\r\n    if commits is None:\r\n        return [\"cannot list the squashed commits {}..{}\".format(head[:10], tip[:10])]\r\n    return [\r\n        line\r\n        for rev in commits.split()\r\n        for line in commit_adoption_record_lines(root, rev)\r\n_show = _kitgit.git_show\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _tree_specs(root, rev):\r\n    \"\"\"`{WI id: path}` of every spec in a tree (`rev` None is the index), or\r\n    None when git cannot list it. A `-000` example is never a row.\r\n\r\n    Implements: SR-148, LLR-298\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    roots = [WORK_DIR, ARCHIVE_WORK_DIR]\r\n    if rev is None:\r\n        paths = _git_paths(root, [\"ls-files\", \"--\"] + roots)\r\n    else:\r\n        paths = _git_paths(root, [\"ls-tree\", \"-r\", \"--name-only\", rev, \"--\"] + roots)\r\n    if paths is None:\r\n        return None\r\n    specs = {}\r\n    for path in paths:\r\n        name = path.rsplit(\"/\", 1)[-1]\r\n        wid = \"-\".join(name.split(\"-\")[:2])\r\n        if name.startswith(\"WI-\") and name.endswith(\".md\") and not wid.endswith(\"-000\"):\r\n            specs[wid] = path\r\n    return specs\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _registry_states(root, prefix):\r\n    \"\"\"`{OI id: status}` of the open-items registry in ONE tree (`prefix` a\r\n    `git show` prefix), read through whichever carrier that tree uses (TOML,\r\n    else CSV — `spine_carrier`'s resolution, as `_spine_rows_at` reads a spine\r\n    registry): `{}` when the tree has neither, None when it does not parse.\r\n    `-000` example rows are dropped.\r\n\r\n    Implements: SR-148, LLR-298\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    for cand in _spine_carriers(OPEN_ITEMS_REGISTRY):\r\n        text = _show(root, prefix, cand)\r\n        if text is None:\r\n            continue\r\n        rows = spine_carrier.rows_from_text(text, \"OI-ID\", \".\" + cand.rsplit(\".\", 1)[1])\r\n        if rows is None:\r\n            return None\r\n        return {\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"@'\nimport subprocess,tomllib\nrev='8d210ce585b92f6dcdedcdecda4c132bda5e6cbb'\ndata=tomllib.loads(subprocess.check_output(['git','show',f'{rev}:docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.toml']).decode())\nids={'SN-025','SN-029','SN-027','SN-006','SN-008','SN-010'}\ndef visit(x):\n if isinstance(x,dict):\n  for k,v in x.items():\n   if k in ids: print(k,v)\n   else: visit(v)\n elif isinstance(x,list):\n  for v in x: visit(v)\nvisit(data)\n'@ | python -\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"@'\nimport subprocess,tomllib\nrev='8d210ce585b92f6dcdedcdecda4c132bda5e6cbb'\ndata=tomllib.loads(subprocess.check_output(['git','show',f'{rev}:docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.toml']).decode())\nids={'SN-025','SN-029','SN-027','SN-006','SN-008','SN-010'}\ndef visit(x):\n if isinstance(x,dict):\n  for k,v in x.items():\n   if k in ids: print(k,v)\n   else: visit(v)\n elif isinstance(x,list):\n  for v in x: visit(v)\nvisit(data)\n'@ | python -\"","aggregated_output":"SN-006 {'status': 'Approved', 'stakeholder_refs': ['STK-01'], 'tags': ['unattended', 'loop', 'scripts', 'personal-data'], 'need': \"An agent can run **unattended** and resume from repository text alone. Such a run never waits for interactive input, and reports its failures clearly. It also stays **safe**: declared limits bound the workers it may set running and the actions it takes that cannot be undone, and only an override a human provides � never one the model can set � may relax those limits. That safety is **supervision rather than an absolute**: the layer's first obligation is to move forward as far as it can, and it surfaces to a human when it cannot proceed � so a fault in the machinery that carries a limit degrades to a recorded condition, not to a stopped run.\", 'why': 'Agents develop here, and a walk-away run is only worth having if it can be both left alone and trusted while unwatched. The safety half is stated in the need itself rather than only here, because a `why` cell is context that nothing derives from: a safety obligation recorded only here leaves the gate enforcing it parentless, and drops out of any re-derivation of the need.', 'priority': 'S', 'acceptance': \"The unattended loop resumes from the repository's tracked status surface, exits a **typed code** at each end state, and a preflight refuses a broken footing (no agent CLI, not a git repo, private author under privacy-check) rather than hanging. During unattended operation, configured limits bound every spawned worker and irreversible action, and only an explicit human-provided override may exceed those limits. A fault in a limit's own enforcement is recorded and lets the run continue rather than halting it � the run surfaces to a human when it cannot proceed, never when its own supervision errs.\"}\r\nSN-008 {'status': 'Approved', 'stakeholder_refs': ['STK-03'], 'need': 'A reader can believe a **pass verdict**: gates are **honest**, and a pass verdict never hides a skipped check, a stub, or an unmet declared criterion.', 'why': \"*Trust what ships*; a false pass is worse than a failure. The verdict is named as a verdict � the word PASS � never by hue: a colour word as the only name for the system's most important signal does not exist for a substantial class of readers, and the kit's console output carries zero ANSI colour in any case.\", 'priority': 'M', 'acceptance': 'The harness **fails** (not skips) on a missing required tool � the explicitly-requested `--lenient` local mode is the one sanctioned degrade to SKIP (SR-006), never a CI/gate default; the no-stub detector and the privacy/secrets floor run at their declared gate.'}\r\nSN-010 {'status': 'Approved', 'stakeholder_refs': ['STK-03'], 'need': 'A reader can navigate the documentation and trust it: **navigable and honest** � links resolve, the vision is declared once, and generated views cannot silently rot.', 'why': '*Readable and correct over the long run.*', 'priority': 'S', 'acceptance': 'The documentation check fails on a broken intra-repo link or a missing `PROJECT-VISION` tag; every generated artifact carries a `--check` freshness contract.'}\r\nSN-025 {'status': 'Approved', 'stakeholder_refs': ['STK-01'], 'tags': ['unattended', 'loop'], 'need': \"**With no human curating what comes next**, a configured LLM agent implements toward the vision � fully autonomously where enabled � deriving its own next work from the repository's tracked state. (The launcher that starts it is SN-034's obligation; this need is the self-direction.)\", 'why': 'Walk-away autonomy is the whole promise of the unattended layer: if a human must hand-maintain a next-step pointer, the loop is attended in practice and the pointer rots against the registry it paraphrases. Extends SN-006 (a *resumable* unattended run) into a *self-directing* one.', 'priority': 'S', 'acceptance': 'A plain launch derives what to do next from the tracked WI DAG plus Git � never from prose or a hand-maintained pointer, and never from predefined tracks; the ready frontier is ordered deterministically, so two readers of the same registry dispatch the same work; the status surface a human reads is generated from that tracked state, never hand-copied.'}\r\nSN-027 {'status': 'Approved', 'stakeholder_refs': ['STK-02'], 'tags': ['perf', 'unattended', 'scripts'], 'need': 'A team gets more than one piece of ready work moving at once: ready work **fans out across bounded parallel lanes**, while mutation of the integration branch stays **serialized and gated**.', 'why': 'Concurrency is admitted here on structural grounds, not throughput ones: the WI DAG already encodes what may proceed, so a frontier bounded to one lane idles ready work for no reason; and parallel *integration* is precisely how a reviewed process ships an unreviewed tree � so fan-out is admitted only because it narrows back to a single serialized, gated seam before anything lands. No instrument here measures speedup, so a quantified-speedup justification would be unfalsifiable as written; the observability such a claim would need is carried instead by the lane-utilisation report.', 'priority': 'S', 'acceptance': 'A launch with independent ready WIs runs up to the configured worker ceiling (default 2) concurrently in isolated worktrees; every finished branch lands through one serial, fail-closed integrator that runs the declared bar on the composed tree; `--jobs 1` preserves the serial semantic; a declared pause stops claiming and drains what is already in flight; a crash at any lifecycle boundary recovers from Git history alone, without double-assignment or half-integrated authoritative state. Spec of record: `docs/archive/specs/parallel-wi-dispatch.2026-07-20.md` + `docs/concurrency-restructure.md`.'}\r\nSN-029 {'status': 'Approved', 'stakeholder_refs': ['STK-01'], 'tags': ['unattended', 'loop'], 'need': \"**An autonomous run gets as far as it honestly can.** Once triggered, the coordinator stops for a human judgement only when the declared approval level reserves that tier for a human, when a round cannot converge on its own, or when requirement/test documentation is introduced or amended such that the gate drops below what automation is permitted to attest. On every tier released to automation, an approval still happens as a review from a declared perspective against the row's actual evidence, and leaves a record naming who or what approved it � releasing a tier means the review is delegated, never skipped.\", 'why': 'The unattended layer only pays for itself if a walk-away run actually walks away � so what stops it must be exactly the set of judgements the owner reserved, and nothing else. A three-value gate-authority enum (`attended` / `single-approve` / `autonomous`) cannot express \"TCs are human-held but LLRs are not\", the distinction an owner actually wants to dial, and leaves each consuming table free to re-interpret the same word with its own fail-safe direction. Answering \"has this been approved?\" by walking git for the newest commit where a row reads `Verified` is sound only while every amendment flips its row in the same commit � precisely the sanctioned path an amendment detector ignores � so the blessed way to amend an attested requirement is invisible to every consumer, and a stakeholder need has no anchor at all (an SN has no Status cell).', 'priority': 'S', 'acceptance': \"A cumulative level names the highest tier a human still approves, compared against a separately derived SPINE STAGE (which tier is in process) via a declared, auditable mapping. Every failure direction � an unreadable stage, an out-of-range level, a wrong-typed dial � resolves toward MORE human involvement, because the failure that matters is a machine approving something a human meant to hold. Each acceptance is anchored ON THE ACCEPTED ARTIFACT'S OWN ROW � the commit whose tree carries the accepted text, plus a digest of that row's normative cells � never in a second registry keyed on the same artifact. Text that has moved away from what was accepted surfaces regardless of any Status movement, the one signal the sanctioned amend-and-flip path would otherwise hide, so an AMENDED requirement drops the derived stage exactly as a newly introduced one does. Each automated approval on a released tier appends a durable record (row, transition, acting reviewer, commit) distinguishable from a human approval by query.\"}\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
